Subject: wayland: deny desktop-shell globals to sandboxed clients unless granted

KWin refuses a client that connected through a wp_security_context_v1
listener seven globals and offers it every other one, the layer shell, output
management, DPMS, the Plasma shell surface, the keyboard state, screen edges,
virtual desktop management and external brightness among them. A sandboxed
client draws its own windows; those globals reach the rest of the desktop.

This refuses them as well, and lets a sandbox open named sets of them again
through grants: window-list, fake-input, screencast, data-control,
layer-shell, output-management, dpms and plasma-shell. The name
input-method is accepted and opens nothing: the input-method globals stay
with the input method KWin starts, as they are for every client. The
security context's sandbox engine and instance id are kept on the client
connection beside its app id, which is what a grant source needs to know
which sandbox a client belongs to. The one source here is KDOS's box
profiles, read for the engine io.kdos.appbox from
~/.config/kdos/boxes/<instance-id>.conf (the `grant` key) once per client;
a client of any other engine is granted nothing. wp_security_context_manager_v1
and kde_lockscreen_overlay_v1 are never granted.

